12
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:06,000
Shooting Dogs, released in the United States as Beyond the Gates, is a 2005 film directed by
Michael Caton-Jones and starring John Hurt, Hugh Dancy and Clare-Hope Ashitey.

13
01:05:00,000 --> 01:05:06,000
It is based on the experiences of BBC news producer David Belton,
who worked in Rwanda during the Rwandan genocide.

14
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
Belton is the film's co-writer and one of its producers.

15
01:15:00,000 --> 01:15:06,000
The setting of the film is the École Technique Officielle (ETO) in
Kigali, Rwanda, in 1994, during the Rwandan genocide.

16
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:06,000
Hurt plays a Catholic priest (loosely based on Vjekoslav Ćurić) and Dancy an English
teacher, both Europeans, who are caught up in the events of the genocide.

17
01:25:00,000 --> 01:25:06,000
Unlike Hotel Rwanda, which was filmed in South Africa using South African actors,
the film was shot in the original location of the scenes it portrays.

18
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:06,000
Also, many of the massacre survivors were employed as part of
the production crew and in minor acting roles.

19
01:35:00,000 --> 01:35:06,000
The film's title refers to the actions of UN soldiers in shooting at
the stray dogs that scavenged the bodies of the dead.

20
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:06,000
Since the UN soldiers were not allowed to shoot at the Hutu extremists who had caused the deaths in the first place,
the shooting of dogs is symbolic of the madness of the situation that the film attempts to capture.

21
01:45:00,000 --> 01:45:06,000
The film explores themes of destruction of a
civilization, rape, civil war, hutu, tutsi.

22
01:50:00,000 --> 01:50:06,000
The tone is emotional.
